-- 044: Deep content + matching exercises for X08 · Python/Pandas 101.
UPDATE public.sessions SET
 content_en = E'## Why an analyst learns Python\n\nSpreadsheets and SQL take you far, but Python (with **pandas**) handles data too big or too repetitive for either — and it automates the boring parts. If you do the same clean-and-report every week, Python does it in one click.\n\nRun every snippet in **Playground → Python** as you read.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Setup & basics\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd            # the analyst''s core library\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(''sales.csv'')  # load a CSV into a DataFrame\n# pd.read_excel(''sales.xlsx'') for Excel files\n```\n\n| Structure | What it is |\n|-----------|-----------|\n| **DataFrame** | A 2-D table (rows × columns), like a sheet |\n| **Series** | A single column of a DataFrame |\n| **dtype** | A column''s data type (int64, float64, object) |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Exploring a DataFrame\n\n```python\ndf.head()          # first 5 rows\ndf.info()          # columns, types, non-null counts\ndf.describe()      # summary stats for numeric columns\n\ndf[''revenue''].sum()                       # total\ndf[df[''revenue''] > 1000000]               # filter rows\ndf.groupby(''category'')[''revenue''].sum()   # aggregate by group\n```\n\n| Task | Code |\n|------|------|\n| First rows | `df.head()` |\n| Column total | `df[''revenue''].sum()` |\n| Filter rows | `df[df[''revenue''] > 1000000]` |\n| Group & aggregate | `df.groupby(''category'')[''revenue''].sum()` |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Charts with Matplotlib & Seaborn\n\n```python\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport seaborn as sns\n\nrevenue_by_cat = df.groupby(''category'')[''revenue''].sum()\nrevenue_by_cat.plot(kind=''bar'')      # quick Matplotlib bar\nplt.title(''Revenue by Category'')\nplt.show()\n\nsns.histplot(df[''revenue''])          # Seaborn distribution\n```\nMatplotlib is the engine; Seaborn is a friendlier layer for statistical charts.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Automating Excel reports (openpyxl)\n\n```python\n# pandas can write straight to Excel via openpyxl\nsummary = df.groupby(''category'')[''revenue''].sum().reset_index()\nsummary.to_excel(''report.xlsx'', index=False)\n```\nRun this on a schedule and the weekly report writes itself — the essence of automation.\n\n---\n\n> **Mini case study.** A monthly stock report meant 2 hours of filtering and pasting. A 15-line pandas script now reads the raw export, groups by category, and writes a formatted `report.xlsx` in seconds — same output, zero manual steps.\n\n---\n\n## Common pitfalls\n\n- **`SettingWithCopyWarning`** — modify a real copy with `.copy()`, not a slice.\n- **Numbers read as text** — check `df.info()`; convert with `pd.to_numeric(...)`.\n- **Chained filters without brackets** — wrap conditions: `df[(df.a>1) & (df.b<2)]`.\n- **Forgetting `index=False`** on `to_excel` — you get an unwanted extra column.\n\n---\n\n## Glossary\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|------|---------|\n| pandas | The core Python data-analysis library |\n| DataFrame | A 2-D labelled table |\n| Series | One column of a DataFrame |\n| groupby | Split-apply-combine aggregation |\n| Matplotlib / Seaborn | Plotting libraries |\n| openpyxl | Reads/writes Excel files |\n\n---\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n1. `import pandas as pd`, then `read_csv`/`read_excel` load data into a **DataFrame**.\n2. Explore with `head()`, `info()`, `describe()`; a single column is a **Series**.\n3. Filter with boolean masks; summarise with `groupby(...).sum()`.\n4. **Matplotlib/Seaborn** plot; **openpyxl** (via `to_excel`) writes reports.\n5. Python''s payoff is **automation** — write once, run every week.\n\n> 💡 Run the snippets in Playground → Python, then complete the matching exercises below.',
 content_id = E'## Kenapa analyst belajar Python\n\nSpreadsheet dan SQL membawa Anda jauh, tapi Python (dengan **pandas**) menangani data yang terlalu besar atau terlalu berulang untuk keduanya — dan mengotomasi bagian yang membosankan. Kalau Anda melakukan clean-and-report yang sama tiap minggu, Python melakukannya dalam satu klik.\n\nJalankan tiap cuplikan di **Playground → Python** sambil membaca.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Setup & dasar\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd            # library inti analyst\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(''sales.csv'')  # muat CSV ke DataFrame\n# pd.read_excel(''sales.xlsx'') untuk file Excel\n```\n\n| Struktur | Apa itu |\n|----------|---------|\n| **DataFrame** | Tabel 2-D (baris × kolom), seperti sheet |\n| **Series** | Satu kolom dari DataFrame |\n| **dtype** | Tipe data kolom (int64, float64, object) |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Mengeksplorasi DataFrame\n\n```python\ndf.head()          # 5 baris pertama\ndf.info()          # kolom, tipe, jumlah non-null\ndf.describe()      # statistik ringkas kolom numerik\n\ndf[''revenue''].sum()                       # total\ndf[df[''revenue''] > 1000000]               # filter baris\ndf.groupby(''category'')[''revenue''].sum()   # agregasi per grup\n```\n\n| Tugas | Kode |\n|-------|------|\n| Baris pertama | `df.head()` |\n| Total kolom | `df[''revenue''].sum()` |\n| Filter baris | `df[df[''revenue''] > 1000000]` |\n| Group & agregasi | `df.groupby(''category'')[''revenue''].sum()` |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Chart dengan Matplotlib & Seaborn\n\n```python\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport seaborn as sns\n\nrevenue_by_cat = df.groupby(''category'')[''revenue''].sum()\nrevenue_by_cat.plot(kind=''bar'')      # bar cepat Matplotlib\nplt.title(''Revenue per Kategori'')\nplt.show()\n\nsns.histplot(df[''revenue''])          # distribusi Seaborn\n```\nMatplotlib adalah mesinnya; Seaborn lapisan yang lebih ramah untuk chart statistik.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Otomasi laporan Excel (openpyxl)\n\n```python\n# pandas bisa menulis langsung ke Excel via openpyxl\nsummary = df.groupby(''category'')[''revenue''].sum().reset_index()\nsummary.to_excel(''report.xlsx'', index=False)\n```\nJalankan terjadwal dan laporan mingguan menulis dirinya sendiri — inti dari automation.\n\n---\n\n> **Studi kasus mini.** Laporan stok bulanan berarti 2 jam memfilter dan menempel. Skrip pandas 15 baris kini membaca ekspor mentah, mengelompokkan per kategori, dan menulis `report.xlsx` terformat dalam hitungan detik — output sama, nol langkah manual.\n\n---\n\n## Kesalahan umum\n\n- **`SettingWithCopyWarning`** — ubah salinan asli dengan `.copy()`, bukan slice.\n- **Angka terbaca sebagai teks** — cek `df.info()`; konversi dengan `pd.to_numeric(...)`.\n- **Filter berantai tanpa kurung** — bungkus kondisi: `df[(df.a>1) & (df.b<2)]`.\n- **Lupa `index=False`** pada `to_excel` — muncul kolom ekstra yang tak diinginkan.\n\n---\n\n## Glosarium\n\n| Istilah | Arti |\n|---------|------|\n| pandas | Library inti analisis data Python |\n| DataFrame | Tabel 2-D berlabel |\n| Series | Satu kolom DataFrame |\n| groupby | Agregasi split-apply-combine |\n| Matplotlib / Seaborn | Library plotting |\n| openpyxl | Membaca/menulis file Excel |\n\n---\n\n## Kesimpulan Utama\n\n1. `import pandas as pd`, lalu `read_csv`/`read_excel` memuat data ke **DataFrame**.\n2. Eksplorasi dengan `head()`, `info()`, `describe()`; satu kolom adalah **Series**.\n3. Filter dengan boolean mask; ringkas dengan `groupby(...).sum()`.\n4. **Matplotlib/Seaborn** untuk plot; **openpyxl** (via `to_excel`) menulis laporan.\n5. Nilai lebih Python adalah **automation** — tulis sekali, jalankan tiap minggu.\n\n> 💡 Jalankan cuplikan di Playground → Python, lalu selesaikan exercise matching di bawah.'
WHERE session_number = 'X08';

